::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which speech product follows accepted format expectations?
{
~A. A page of unrelated facts with no sections.#This does not show a clear format.
=B. An outline with introduction, focus, main points, support, transitions, and conclusion.#Correct. This follows a complete speech structure.
~C. A note that says "talk about school."#This is too vague and incomplete.
~D. A conclusion only.#A conclusion alone is not a complete speech product.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which note would best help a speaker present information orally?
{
~A. Say stuff about notes.#This is vague and not useful for organized speaking.
~B. Read every sentence from source.#This may lead to reading instead of presenting.
~C. Something about studying maybe.#This does not give a clear speech cue.
=D. Point 2: headings help students find sections quickly.#Correct. This note gives a clear point for delivery.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.04
Question:
Which sentence is conventionally correct?
{
=A. My presentation includes three organized main points.#Correct. The sentence uses standard capitalization and grammar.
~B. my presentation include three organized main points.#This has capitalization and agreement errors.
~C. My presentation include three organized point groups.#This has a subject-verb agreement error.
~D. my presentation includes three organized points.#This needs capitalization at the beginning before it follows standard English conventions.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
What does format help a speaker do?
{
~A. Hide the speech focus.#Format should make the focus clearer, not hide it.
~B. Avoid planning the speech.#Format is part of planning.
=C. Arrange speech parts so the message is clear.#Correct. Format supports clear communication.
~D. Replace every main point with a joke.#This weakens quality and organization.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which item is missing from this outline: introduction, focus, three points, support, transitions?
{
=A. Conclusion#Correct. A complete speech outline needs a conclusion to close the message.
~B. More unrelated facts#Unrelated facts do not complete the required format.
~C. Random title words#A title may help, but the missing major speech part is the conclusion.
~D. A longer font#Font length is not a speech format requirement.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which outline choice supports coherent oral presentation?
{
~A. Put main points in random order.#Random order makes the speech harder to follow.
=B. Arrange main points in a logical sequence.#Correct. Logical order helps the audience follow the speech.
~C. Remove all transitions.#Transitions help listeners follow connections.
~D. Add unrelated details between points.#Unrelated details weaken focus.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.04
Question:
Which revision fixes the sentence "my speech describe two types of transitions"?
{
~A. my speech describe transition types.#This keeps the original capitalization and agreement errors.
~B. My speech describe two types of transitions.#This fixes capitalization but not subject-verb agreement.
=C. My speech describes two types of transitions.#Correct. This fixes capitalization and agreement.
~D. my speech describes two transition types.#This still needs capitalization before it is ready for speech notes.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which checklist item best measures quality?
{
~A. The speech product has many words.#Many words do not guarantee clear communication.
~B. The conclusion introduces a new topic.#A conclusion should close the focus, not shift topics.
~C. The notes are hard to use while speaking.#Hard-to-use notes do not meet quality expectations.
=D. Each transition shows how ideas connect.#Correct. Useful transitions are a quality feature.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
What is the best reason to label parts of a speech outline?
{
=A. Labels make the structure easier to follow and check.#Correct. Labels help the speaker see whether the format is complete.
~B. Labels replace the need for support.#Support is still needed under main points.
~C. Labels make every idea correct automatically.#Labels help organization but do not guarantee content accuracy.
~D. Labels hide missing sections.#Labels should reveal missing sections, not hide them.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which note card is strongest for delivery?
{
~A. "Everything about planning."#This is too broad to guide delivery.
~B. "Read entire paragraph."#This may cause the speaker to read instead of present.
=C. "Point 1: planner lists deadlines; explain why visible deadlines help."#Correct. This gives a clear cue and a reasoning reminder.
~D. "Something next."#This is vague and not useful.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.04
Question:
Which sentence needs correction?
{
~A. My outline includes transitions between the main points.#This sentence uses standard English.
=B. my outline include transitions between the main points.#Correct. This needs capitalization and subject-verb agreement correction.
~C. The conclusion restates the focus of the speech.#This sentence is correct.
~D. Each point includes support and reasoning.#This sentence is correct.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which revision improves a weak speech product?
{
~A. Remove all headings from the outline.#Removing headings makes structure less clear.
~B. Delete the focus sentence.#The focus sentence is needed for organization.
=C. Add support under each main point.#Correct. Support helps the speech product meet quality expectations.
~D. Add unrelated stories to every section.#Unrelated stories weaken the format and focus.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which statement best describes quality expectations?
{
=A. Standards that help work become complete, clear, accurate, and ready for its purpose.#Correct. Quality expectations guide how well the product works.
~B. A rule that every speech must be as long as possible.#Length alone is not quality.
~C. A way to avoid checking grammar.#Conventions are part of quality.
~D. A reason to remove organization.#Organization supports quality.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Why should a speech outline include transitions?
{
~A. To make the speech less organized.#Transitions make the speech more organized.
=B. To guide listeners from one idea to the next.#Correct. Transitions support oral presentation by showing connections.
~C. To replace the conclusion.#A conclusion is still needed.
~D. To add unrelated facts.#Transitions should connect related ideas.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.04
Question:
Which correction is best for "the speaker use note cards"?
{
~A. the speaker use note cards#This keeps the original errors.
~B. The speaker use note cards.#This fixes capitalization but not agreement.
=C. The speaker uses note cards.#Correct. This uses capitalization and correct subject-verb agreement.
~D. the speaker uses note-card cues.#This fixes agreement but not capitalization.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which speech product is incomplete?
{
~A. Outline with focus, main points, support, transitions, and conclusion.#This includes major required parts.
~B. Note card with a clear point and evidence cue.#This can be a useful speech product.
~C. Checklist with format, support, transitions, and conventions.#This checks multiple quality expectations.
=D. Outline with only a title and no speech sections.#Correct. A title alone is incomplete.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which question should a speaker ask when checking format?
{
=A. Does my product include the required speech parts?#Correct. Format checking begins with required parts.
~B. Can I remove the focus?#Removing the focus weakens format and organization.
~C. Did I add unrelated material?#Unrelated material is a problem, not a format goal.
~D. Is my work confusing enough?#Quality expectations require clarity.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which conclusion best supports a quality speech format?
{
~A. I am done now, and I forgot my topic.#This does not close the speech focus.
=B. Planning ahead helps students manage deadlines, materials, and study time.#Correct. This restates the central idea and closes the speech.
~C. Here is a new topic I did not mention earlier.#A conclusion should not introduce an unrelated topic.
~D. My speech had some words in it.#This is vague and does not summarize the focus.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.04
Question:
Which sentence is ready for submission?
{
~A. my note cards helps presentation delivery.#This has capitalization and agreement errors.
~B. My note cards helps me present.#This has a subject-verb agreement error.
=C. My note cards help me present.#Correct. This sentence follows standard English conventions.
~D. my note cards support my presentation.#This needs capitalization before it follows standard English conventions.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
What is the best revision for a note card that says "say all the things"?
{
~A. Keep it because it is short.#Short notes must still be useful.
~B. Add more vague words.#Vague wording does not improve quality.
~C. Delete every cue from the card.#The speaker still needs useful cues.
=D. Replace it with a specific point and support cue.#Correct. A note card should guide the speaker clearly.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which item belongs on a speech quality checklist?
{
=A. Main points are in logical order.#Correct. Logical order is part of quality speech organization.
~B. Every sentence is copied from a source.#Copying is not a quality expectation for speech notes.
~C. The outline avoids a conclusion.#A conclusion is usually required.
~D. The speaker uses no support.#Support is needed for quality development.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which choice best supports coherent focus in a speech product?
{
=A. Each main point supports the same focus.#Correct. Coherent focus means the parts work together.
~B. Each main point changes to a new topic.#Changing topics weakens coherence.
~C. The conclusion ignores the focus.#A conclusion should return to the focus.
~D. The outline includes unrelated facts.#Unrelated facts break focus.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.04
Question:
What should a speaker check before submitting speech notes?
{
~A. Whether errors make the notes harder to understand.#This is a useful concern, but the best answer names the convention check directly.
=B. Whether capitalization, spelling, punctuation, and grammar are correct.#Correct. These are standard English conventions.
~C. Whether all words are lowercase.#All-lowercase writing is not standard English.
~D. Whether the notes avoid punctuation.#Punctuation supports clarity.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which format problem should be fixed first?
{
~A. The outline has one extra clear support detail.#An extra relevant detail may not be the biggest format problem.
~B. The note card uses a precise academic word.#Precise academic words can improve quality.
~C. The introduction has a clear focus statement.#This is a strength, not a problem.
=D. The outline has no main points.#Correct. Main points are essential speech format parts.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_QZ_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which statement best summarizes this lesson?
{
~A. Quality speech products should avoid transitions.#Transitions support organization and quality.
~B. Quality speech products only need to be long.#Length alone does not show quality.
~C. Quality speech products do not need checking.#Checking is part of producing polished work.
=D. Quality speech products follow the required format, organize ideas clearly, and use standard English conventions.#Correct. This summarizes format-specific quality work.
}
